        "timestamp": 1778827517,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement Britain This Week in Asia Health & Environment Greening of UK politics could mean big business for Asian renewable exporters The Green Party’s record-breaking gains in UK council elections are a sign voters are taking climate change seriously, analysts say 3 -MIN READ 3 -MIN Listen Biman Mukherji Published: 2:45pm, 15 May 2026 South and Southeast Asian nations may emerge as unexpected clean energy beneficiaries after the Green Party of England and Wales made record-breaking gains in recent UK local council elections, according to analysts. The Greens expanded their footprint in the May 7 council elections, capitalising on widespread voter disillusionment with Britain’s two major parties, Labour and Conservative . It was the party’s best-ever performance in a local election. While Nigel Farage’s right-wing populist Reform UK party was the largest numerical winner, sweeping conservative and northern working-class areas, the climate-conscious Green Party established itself as the main alternative for left-leaning voters. Advertisement “The UK voter no longer views climate change as a distant threat; they see it through the lens of their soaring energy bills and the recurrent flooding of their local high streets,” said Harjeet Singh, climate activist and founding director of Satat Sampada Climate Foundation. “The Green Party’s historic wins demonstrate that the public is finally connecting the dots,” he said. The results of the council elections will have immediate consequences for the business of government, with huge numbers of incumbent Labour and Conservative politicians replaced by new councillors. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
